  "name": "Miramichi lumberwoods song (Maritime Anglo-Canadian balladry)",
  "family": "global",
  "lineage": "English-language ballad and song tradition of New Brunswick and Nova Scotia, sung in lumber camps, on river drives and at kitchen gatherings, mixing old British broadside and Child ballads with locally made songs about logging deaths, drownings and local events by woods poets such as Larry Gorman and Joe Scott. Louise Manny founded the Miramichi Folksong Festival in 1958, whose singers were recorded for Folkways, and Helen Creighton had collected the Nova Scotia repertoire since 1928.",
